EXPECTED OUTPUT (AlpacaEval):
Technical debt is like taking out a loan to buy a house. If you need a place to live immediately, you might take out a mortgage (a quick software fix or shortcut) rather than waiting years to save up the full cash amount (building the perfect, most robust software solution). While this gets you into the house faster, you now have to pay interest. In software, that 'interest' is the extra time and effort it takes to add new features or fix bugs later because the code is messy or poorly structured. If you don't periodically pay down the principal by refactoring and cleaning up the code, the interest becomes so overwhelming that development grinds to a halt, just like going bankrupt from too much financial debt.

